---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { CAL_URL, EMAIL, LINKEDIN_URL, SITE_DESCRIPTION, SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
import '../styles/home.css';
---

<BaseLayout title={SITE_TITLE} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION}>
  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="status"><span class="dot"></span> Available — new advisory &amp; fractional engagements</div>
      <h1>Fractional CTO &amp; technical advisor for <span class="mark">AI startups</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        I help early-stage founders turn an AI idea into a system that ships, scales, and survives diligence — senior
        technical leadership without the full-time hire. 15 years building production systems — today Senior Backend
        Engineer at HubSpot and part-time CTO at an AI company, previously Amazon and Yandex.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#contact">Book an intro call →</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={LINKEDIN_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">View LinkedIn</a>
      </div>

      <div class="logos">
        <span class="k">Previously:</span>
        <span class="co">Amazon</span> /
        <span class="co">Bird</span> /
        <span class="co">Toptal</span> /
        <span class="co">Yandex</span>
        &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="now">Now: HubSpot · sky-tech.ai</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SERVICES -->
  <section id="services">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <span class="eyebrow"><b>01</b> — What I do</span>
      <h2>Senior technical leadership, on the terms your stage needs.</h2>
      <p>
        Most early AI startups don't need a full-time CTO yet — they need someone senior who has shipped real systems to
        make the right calls before they become expensive. That's where I come in.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="num">01</span>
        <h3>Technical strategy &amp; architecture</h3>
        <p>
          Translate the product vision into a pragmatic architecture and roadmap. Choose the stack, the boundaries, and
          the trade-offs that won't box you in at the next stage.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="num">02</span>
        <h3>From prototype to production</h3>
        <p>
          Take an LLM demo or notebook and turn it into a reliable, observable, secure product — evals, guardrails,
          latency and cost under control, not just a happy-path demo.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="num">03</span>
        <h3>Hands-on building</h3>
        <p>
          I still write code. When the team is small, I build the hard parts myself — APIs, data pipelines, infra — and
          set the patterns the rest of the codebase follows.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="num">04</span>
        <h3>Cloud, infra &amp; DevOps</h3>
        <p>
          AWS, Terraform/CDK, CI/CD, and observability done properly from day one. Ship fast without accumulating the
          kind of debt that stalls you six months in.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="num">05</span>
        <h3>Team &amp; hiring</h3>
        <p>
          Define the first engineering hires, interview and calibrate candidates, and set up the autonomous,
          DevOps-minded team culture that lets a small group move fast.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="num">06</span>
        <h3>Technical due diligence</h3>
        <p>
          A clear-eyed read on an architecture, codebase, or AI claims — for founders preparing to raise, or for
          investors who need a credible technical assessment.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ENGAGEMENT MODELS -->
  <section id="engage">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <span class="eyebrow"><b>02</b> — How we work together</span>
      <h2>Flexible engagements that match your runway.</h2>
      <p>
        Start small, scale up if it's working. No long lock-ins — the goal is to make myself progressively less
        necessary.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="engage">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="mode">Advisory</div>
        <h3>The sounding board</h3>
        <div class="meta">~a few hours / week</div>
        <ul>
          <li>Architecture &amp; stack reviews</li>
          <li>Weekly strategy sessions</li>
          <li>On-call for the big decisions</li>
          <li>Hiring &amp; interview support</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card feat">
        <div class="mode">Fractional CTO</div>
        <h3>Embedded leadership</h3>
        <div class="meta">1–3 days / week</div>
        <ul>
          <li>Own the technical roadmap</li>
          <li>Lead &amp; mentor the team</li>
          <li>Hands-on with the hard parts</li>
          <li>Represent tech to investors</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="mode">Project</div>
        <h3>Build the foundation</h3>
        <div class="meta">Fixed scope</div>
        <ul>
          <li>0→1 MVP or core system</li>
          <li>LLM feature productionization</li>
          <li>Cloud / infra setup</li>
          <li>Technical due diligence</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- WORK / TRACK RECORD -->
  <section id="work">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <span class="eyebrow"><b>03</b> — Track record</span>
      <h2>Fifteen years of shipping systems that hold up.</h2>
      <p>
        From a single-page app serving 2.5M visits a day at Yandex, through advertising reporting at Amazon, to
        multi-region infrastructure at HubSpot and leading engineering at an AI company today — full ownership, end to
        end.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline">
      <div class="item">
        <div class="when">2025 — Now</div>
        <div>
          <div class="role">Senior Backend Engineer <span class="at">· HubSpot</span></div>
          <div class="where">Edinburgh, UK (remote) · HubSpot Academy</div>
          <p class="desc">
            Led Academy's 13-month migration from hand-written REST APIs to HubSpot's typed RPC framework, deleting ~15k
            lines of legacy code across 37 production changes. Moved the course catalog to serve customers from their own
            region via an asynchronous MySQL binlog pipeline — up to ~78% faster in Asia-Pacific, with zero downtime.
            Built an AI validation agent that checks every practice exercise on create or update and reports pass/fail
            with a reason.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <div class="when">2024 — Now</div>
        <div>
          <div class="role">Chief Technology Officer (part-time) <span class="at">· sky-tech.ai</span></div>
          <div class="where">Edinburgh, UK (remote) · AI document intelligence — medical, insurance &amp; legal</div>
          <p class="desc">
            Lead architecture and engineering for a platform that turns unstructured documents into structured,
            actionable insight — cutting case-review time by up to 70% while keeping workflows HIPAA/GDPR-compliant and
            secure. Own the full stack: LLM pipelines, backend services, cloud infrastructure, and team.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <div class="when">2023 — 2024</div>
        <div>
          <div class="role">Senior Software Engineer <span class="at">· Bird (MessageBird)</span></div>
          <div class="where">Edinburgh, UK (remote) · Full-stack · React / Go</div>
          <p class="desc">
            Built Bird CRM — a unified platform for marketing, sales, and payments — working across a large-scale,
            event-driven microservice architecture serving a global customer base.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <div class="when">2022 — 2023</div>
        <div>
          <div class="role">Senior Software Engineer <span class="at">· Proportunity</span></div>
          <div class="where">Edinburgh, UK (remote) · Fintech start-up · TypeScript / Node.js</div>
          <p class="desc">
            Technology-driven mortgage lender. Built the backend — web crawlers and a GraphQL API in TypeScript/Node.js
            with Postgres on AWS — owning features end-to-end in a small, fast-moving team.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <div class="when">2020 — 2022</div>
        <div>
          <div class="role">Software Development Engineer II <span class="at">· Amazon (Ads)</span></div>
          <div class="where">Edinburgh, UK · Kotlin / React</div>
          <p class="desc">
            Designed and shipped "shareable snapshots" — effectively a time machine for the advertising reporting UI —
            across a Kotlin backend and a React/TypeScript front end at Amazon scale.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <div class="when">2018 — 2019</div>
        <div>
          <div class="role">Front End Engineer <span class="at">· Toptal</span></div>
          <div class="where">Moscow, Russia (remote) · React</div>
          <p class="desc">
            Halved first contentful paint on the home page with an "above the fold" rendering technique, and helped ship
            the new Toptal blog — 1.5× faster page loads.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <div class="when">2013 — 2018</div>
        <div>
          <div class="role">Front End Engineer → Team Lead → Senior Software Engineer <span class="at">· Yandex</span></div>
          <div class="where">Moscow, Russia · JavaScript / Node.js</div>
          <p class="desc">
            Built a single-page app that grew to 2.5M visits a day, along with the in-house JavaScript MVC framework and
            component library behind it. As team lead, grew the front-end team from 2 to 6 and cut feature cycle time.
            Then built an internal CI/CD platform and Node.js CLI tooling for front-end developers.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <div class="when">2011 — 2013</div>
        <div>
          <div class="role">Web Developer → Team Lead <span class="at">· Webstroy</span></div>
          <div class="where">Rostov-on-Don, Russia · HTML / jQuery / PHP</div>
          <p class="desc">
            Where it started: building websites from design to production — PSD to HTML, jQuery and PHP. Within a year,
            led a small team of two developers and ran the projects end to end.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- OPEN SOURCE -->
  <section id="oss">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <span class="eyebrow"><b>04</b> — Open source</span>
      <h2>Side projects, in the open.</h2>
      <p>
        Things I build to learn, to scratch an itch, or just for fun. More on
        <a href="https://github.com/vitkarpov" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid oss">
      <a class="card" href="https://github.com/vitkarpov/tldr" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <span class="num">Go · AWS</span>
        <h3>tldr ↗</h3>
        <p>A Telegram bot that summarizes voice messages and round videos in a funny way. Two Go Lambdas, Amazon Transcribe, and Nova Lite on Bedrock, deployed with Terraform.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href="https://github.com/vitkarpov/relational-formula-engine" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <span class="num">TypeScript</span>
        <h3>relational-formula-engine ↗</h3>
        <p>A formula engine for applications with user-defined objects, typed attributes, and relations between records.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href="https://github.com/vitkarpov/systemdesigntrainer.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <span class="num">TypeScript</span>
        <h3>systemdesigntrainer.com ↗</h3>
        <p>Practice real system design interviews under pressure, before the real one.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href="https://github.com/vitkarpov/vitosik-js" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <span class="num">C++</span>
        <h3>vitosik-js ↗</h3>
        <p>A minimal JavaScript engine written in C++, built to learn how engines work from the inside.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href="https://github.com/vitkarpov/coding-interviews-blog-archive" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <span class="num">1k+ stars</span>
        <h3>coding-interviews-blog-archive ↗</h3>
        <p>Walkthroughs of algorithm problems for Big Tech coding interviews, written in Russian.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href="https://github.com/vitkarpov/jblocks" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <span class="num">JavaScript</span>
        <h3>jblocks ↗</h3>
        <p>A tiny library for building UI components in a declarative way.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ABOUT -->
  <section id="about">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <span class="eyebrow"><b>05</b> — About</span>
      <h2>The best technical leadership makes itself unnecessary.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="about-grid">
      <div class="about-text">
        <p>
          I'm Viktor — a software engineer with <strong>15 years</strong> of building high-quality products and
          untangling hard technical problems. I started in UI and front-end infrastructure (an internal CI/CD platform,
          before GitHub Actions made that mainstream), then moved deep into backend:
          <strong>scalable systems, APIs, and event-driven microservices</strong> — always with performance, reliability,
          and observability front of mind.
        </p>
        <p>
          Today I'm a Senior Backend Engineer at HubSpot and a part-time CTO at an AI company, which means I live the early-stage AI startup reality every day: shipping
          LLM-powered features that have to be accurate, secure, and fast — not just impressive in a demo. I bring that
          same lens to founders as a <strong>fractional CTO and advisor</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          I care about <strong>DevOps culture and fully autonomous teams</strong> — I even ran a podcast about it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack-box">
        <h4>Core stack</h4>
        <div class="chips">
          <span class="chip">Java / Kotlin / Spring</span>
          <span class="chip">Go</span>
          <span class="chip">TypeScript</span>
          <span class="chip">React</span>
          <span class="chip">Node.js</span>
        </div>
        <h4>Infra &amp; AI</h4>
        <div class="chips">
          <span class="chip">AWS</span>
          <span class="chip">Terraform / CDK</span>
          <span class="chip">CI/CD</span>
          <span class="chip">LLM pipelines</span>
          <span class="chip">Observability</span>
        </div>
        <h4>Approach</h4>
        <div class="chips">
          <span class="chip">Event-driven</span>
          <span class="chip">Microservices</span>
          <span class="chip">End-to-end ownership</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section id="contact" class="cta-band">
    <span class="eyebrow"><b>06</b> — Let's talk</span>
    <h2>Building something with AI? Let's make sure it's built right.</h2>
    <p>
      Book a free 30-minute intro call. Tell me where you are and what's keeping you up at night — I'll tell you straight
      whether and how I can help.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={CAL_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Book an intro call →</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>Email me</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
